    8. Synthetic efficiency metrics have been calculated.
    9. Efficiency metric availability has been labeled.
    10. Section 15 remains archive-backed and read-only.

No archive rows have been inserted.
No archive rows have been deleted.
No archive rows have been updated.
No latest-run rows have been modified.
No campaign registry rows have been inserted.
No campaign registry rows have been deleted.
No campaign registry rows have been updated.
No decision outcomes have been recalculated.
No approved exposure has been recalculated.
No approved Expected Loss has been recalculated.
No simulated reason codes have been reassigned.
No BRD or Validation Summary documentation has been drafted.

Next section:
    Section 15.5 should classify each frontier point into an executive-readable diagnostic posture
    label using the summary, baseline-delta, reason-code, and efficiency fields created above.

***************************************************************************************************/



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.5 — FRONTIER CLASSIFICATION
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Assign diagnostic strategy posture labels to each archived frontier point.

    Section 15.5 answers:

        "What kind of strategy tradeoff does this archived frontier point appear to represent?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Drop any prior tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification temporary object.
    2. Create tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification.
    3. Preserve all fields from tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency.
    4. Assign frontier_position_type using transparent baseline-relative delta logic.
    5. Assign frontier_interpretation_note using the same classification logic in plain English.
    6. Display classification output with the supporting metrics needed for review.

INPUT:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency

OUTPUT:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification

GRAIN:
    One row per archived:

        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

WHY THIS SECTION EXISTS:
    Section 15.4 calculates raw frontier and efficiency metrics.

    Raw metrics are necessary, but reviewers also need a compact interpretation layer that can
    support executive discussion.

    Section 15.5 converts numeric movement into diagnostic posture labels such as:
        risk discipline,
        growth orientation,
        exposure efficiency,
        counteroffer management,
        manual-review burden reduction,
        or mixed tradeoff.

CREDIT RISK CONCEPT:
    A strategy frontier point is a tradeoff location.

    A strategy can:
        expand simulated offer exposure,
        reduce approved Expected Loss,
        shift approvals into counteroffers,
        reduce manual-review burden,
        increase final no-offer outcomes,
        or produce mixed results.

    No single metric tells the whole story.

    Section 15.5 labels the most visible tradeoff pattern so the output can be reviewed without
    reading every delta column first.

FRONTIER POSITION TYPES:
    BASELINE_REFERENCE_POINT:
        Baseline strategy under the same Module 1 scenario / population / strategy set.

    NO_BASELINE_REFERENCE_AVAILABLE:
        Strategy slice exists but no matching BASELINE_STRATEGY slice exists for the same frontier
        group, so baseline-relative deltas are unavailable.

    RISK_DISCIPLINE_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy reduces simulated offer exposure and approved Expected Loss while improving the
        offered-population synthetic estimated-risk profile.

    GROWTH_ORIENTED_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy expands simulated offer exposure and approved amount with measurable approved
        Expected Loss tradeoff.

    EXPOSURE_EFFICIENT_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy reduces approved Expected Loss while maintaining or expanding total approved amount.

    COUNTEROFFER_MANAGEMENT_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy shifts outcomes toward reduced-exposure treatment rather than clean approval.

    REVIEW_BURDEN_REDUCTION_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy reduces unresolved manual-review burden by routing more cases to final no-offer
        outcomes.

    BALANCED_OR_MIXED_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Strategy shows mixed tradeoffs requiring segment-level and reason-code review.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    frontier_position_type:
        First-pass diagnostic posture label.

    frontier_interpretation_note:
        Plain-English explanation of why the label was assigned.

    Supporting deltas:
        delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate,
        delta_total_approved_amount,
        delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount,
        delta_approved_expected_loss_rate,
        delta_counteroffer_rate,
        delta_manual_review_rate,
        delta_decline_rate,
        delta_avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    NO_BASELINE_REFERENCE_AVAILABLE:
        Do not interpret baseline-relative deltas. Use absolute frontier metrics only.

    BALANCED_OR_MIXED_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Review segment highlights, reason-code movement, product mix, score mix, approved Expected
        Loss, and counteroffer behavior before drafting executive narrative.

    GROWTH_ORIENTED_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Explain the approved Expected Loss tradeoff explicitly.

    RISK_DISCIPLINE_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Explain access tradeoff and approved Expected Loss reduction explicitly.

    COUNTEROFFER_MANAGEMENT_FRONTIER_POINT:
        Explain whether reduced exposure preserved access or created excessive reliance on
        counteroffers.

CLASSIFICATION BOUNDARY:
    These labels are diagnostic summaries.

    They do not declare a strategy "best."
    They do not recommend production use.
    They do not approve risk appetite.
    They do not replace Section 13 matched comparison.
    They do not replace Section 14 validation.
    They do not represent model validation approval.

DIAGNOSTIC THRESHOLD NOTE:
    Small rate thresholds such as 0.010 are used only to avoid labeling immaterial changes as
    meaningful strategic movement.

    These thresholds are not:
        credit policy thresholds,
        underwriting cutoffs,
        production acceptance thresholds,
        empirical performance thresholds,
        regulatory standards,
        or model validation thresholds.

SQL CONCEPTS TAUGHT IN THIS SECTION:
    CREATE TEMP TABLE ... AS:
        Materializes the classification layer for later Section 15.7 and Section 16 evidence use.

    SELECT e.*:
        Preserves all upstream frontier, baseline-delta, reason-code, and efficiency fields.

    CASE:
        Assigns ordered diagnostic labels. Order matters because the first matching condition wins.

    IS DISTINCT FROM:
        Safely evaluates baseline_available_flag even if NULL is present.

    Baseline-relative deltas:
        Used only after baseline availability is established.

READ-ONLY BOUNDARY:
    Section 15.5 creates a temporary classification helper only.

    It does not modify archive rows.
    It does not modify latest-run rows.
    It does not modify campaign registry rows.
    It does not recalculate decision outcomes.
    It does not recalculate approved exposure.
    It does not recalculate approved Expected Loss.
    It does not reassign simulated reason codes.

***************************************************************************************************/

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification;

CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification
ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS
AS
SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Preserve every field produced by Section 15.4.

    This keeps classification additive:
        Section 15.5 does not recalculate frontier metrics.
        Section 15.5 only adds interpretation fields.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    e.*,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    frontier_position_type.

    SQL mechanics:
        CASE is evaluated from top to bottom.

    Why order matters:
        1. Baseline rows should always be labeled as baseline reference points first.
        2. Missing baseline rows should not be classified using NULL baseline deltas.
        3. More specific posture labels should be evaluated before the residual mixed-tradeoff label.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    CASE
        /*******************************************************************************************
        Baseline row.

        Interpretation:
            This is the reference point for the same strategy set and Module 1 scenario / population.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
            THEN 'BASELINE_REFERENCE_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        No baseline reference.

        Interpretation:
            Absolute metrics can still be reviewed, but baseline-relative movement cannot be
            interpreted.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.baseline_available_flag IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE
            THEN 'NO_BASELINE_REFERENCE_AVAILABLE'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Risk-discipline posture.

        Credit risk interpretation:
            The strategy reduced positive simulated offer exposure, lowered total approved Expected
            Loss, and improved the synthetic risk profile among offer rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate <= -0.010
         AND e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount < 0
         AND e.delta_avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows < 0
            THEN 'RISK_DISCIPLINE_FRONTIER_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Growth-oriented posture.

        Credit risk interpretation:
            The strategy expanded access and approved exposure while accepting an approved Expected
            Loss tradeoff.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate >= 0.010
         AND e.delta_total_approved_amount > 0
         AND e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount >= 0
            THEN 'GROWTH_ORIENTED_FRONTIER_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Exposure-efficient posture.

        Credit risk interpretation:
            The strategy reduced approved Expected Loss while maintaining or increasing total
            approved amount.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount < 0
         AND e.delta_total_approved_amount >= 0
            THEN 'EXPOSURE_EFFICIENT_FRONTIER_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Counteroffer-management posture.

        Credit risk interpretation:
            The strategy shifted more outcomes toward reduced-exposure treatment while reducing or
            not expanding clean approval rate.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.delta_counteroffer_rate >= 0.010
         AND e.delta_approval_rate <= 0
            THEN 'COUNTEROFFER_MANAGEMENT_FRONTIER_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Review-burden-reduction posture.

        Credit risk interpretation:
            The strategy reduced unresolved manual-review outcomes while increasing final no-offer
            outcomes.

        This may be operationally useful but must be explained as a strategy tradeoff.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN e.delta_manual_review_rate <= -0.010
         AND e.delta_decline_rate > 0
            THEN 'REVIEW_BURDEN_REDUCTION_FRONTIER_POINT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Residual mixed label.

        Interpretation:
            The movement pattern does not fit a clean single-posture label. Segment and reason-code
            review should drive the narrative.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        ELSE 'BALANCED_OR_MIXED_FRONTIER_POINT'
    END AS frontier_position_type,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    frontier_interpretation_note.

    SQL mechanics:
        This CASE mirrors the frontier_position_type logic so the label and explanation remain
        aligned.

    Documentation use:
        These notes can be reused in README / executive draft language, but should always be paired
        with the supporting metric columns.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    CASE
        WHEN e.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
            THEN 'Reference point for same Module 1 scenario / population / strategy set.'

        WHEN e.baseline_available_flag IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE
            THEN 'No baseline strategy slice exists for this Module 1 scenario / population / strategy set.'

        WHEN e.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate <= -0.010
         AND e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount < 0
         AND e.delta_avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows < 0
            THEN 'Strategy reduces simulated offer exposure and approved Expected Loss while improving offered-population synthetic estimated-risk profile.'

        WHEN e.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate >= 0.010
         AND e.delta_total_approved_amount > 0
         AND e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount >= 0
            THEN 'Strategy expands simulated offer exposure and approved amount with measurable approved Expected Loss tradeoff.'

        WHEN e.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount < 0
         AND e.delta_total_approved_amount >= 0
            THEN 'Strategy improves approved Expected Loss while maintaining or expanding total approved amount.'

        WHEN e.delta_counteroffer_rate >= 0.010
         AND e.delta_approval_rate <= 0
            THEN 'Strategy shifts outcomes toward reduced-exposure treatment rather than clean approval.'

        WHEN e.delta_manual_review_rate <= -0.010
         AND e.delta_decline_rate > 0
            THEN 'Strategy reduces unresolved manual-review burden by routing more cases to final no-offer outcomes.'

        ELSE 'Strategy shows mixed tradeoffs requiring segment-level and reason-code review.'
    END AS frontier_interpretation_note

FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency e;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15.5A Strategy frontier classification review output.

Business purpose:
    Display each archived frontier point with its diagnostic posture label, plain-English
    interpretation note, and supporting metrics.

What to look for:
    Confirm that frontier_position_type agrees with the supporting deltas.

    Review baseline_available_flag before interpreting deltas.

    Review top_reason_code_delta_driver_code to understand the explanation-code movement behind the
    numeric frontier movement.

Review triggers:
    If a classification appears surprising, review:
        baseline availability,
        delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate,
        delta_total_approved_amount,
        delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount,
        delta_approved_expected_loss_rate,
        delta_counteroffer_rate,
        delta_manual_review_rate,
        delta_decline_rate,
        reason-code movement,
        and product × score segment highlights.

SQL mechanics:
    This query does not calculate new values.

    It selects the most useful classification and supporting metric fields from the temporary
    classification table.

Documentation use:
    This output is the first executive-readable posture layer before Section 15.7 ranks and enriches
    the frontier with registry IDs.

Boundary:
    Classification labels are review aids.

    They support storytelling but do not replace evidence review.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Technical frontier identity.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    strategy_set_name,
    strategy_name,
    strategy_version,

    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Classification output.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    frontier_position_type,
    frontier_interpretation_note,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Absolute outcome rates.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    approval_rate,
    counteroffer_rate,
    manual_review_rate,
    decline_rate,
    simulated_offer_exposure_rate,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Baseline-relative outcome-rate movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    delta_approval_rate,
    delta_counteroffer_rate,
    delta_manual_review_rate,
    delta_decline_rate,
    delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Approved exposure movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    total_approved_amount,
    delta_total_approved_amount,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Approved Expected Loss movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    total_approved_expected_loss_amount,
    delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount,

    approved_expected_loss_rate,
    delta_approved_expected_loss_rate,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Offered-population synthetic risk movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows,
    delta_avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Reason-code story fields.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    top_primary_reason_family,
    top_primary_reason_code,

    top_non_approval_primary_reason_family,
    top_non_approval_primary_reason_code,
    top_non_approval_reason_population_share,

    top_policy_stop_primary_reason_family,
    top_policy_stop_primary_reason_code,
    top_policy_stop_reason_population_share,

    top_reason_code_delta_driver_family,
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_code,
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_baseline_share,
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_strategy_share,
    top_reason_code_delta_population_share

FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification

ORDER BY
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,
    strategy_set_name,
    CASE
        WHEN strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY' THEN 1
        ELSE 2
    END,
    frontier_position_type,
    strategy_name,
    strategy_version;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.6 — SEGMENT-LEVEL FRONTIER HIGHLIGHTS
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Provide product, score-band, and product × score-band frontier summaries across archived slices.

    Section 15.6 answers:

        "Which product, score-band, and product × score-band segments carry the most access,
         exposure, approved Expected Loss, affordability pressure, synthetic risk, or reason-code
         concentration?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Drop any prior tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights.
    2. Create segment-level rows at three grains:
            PRODUCT
            SCORE_BAND
            PRODUCT_X_SCORE
    3. Summarize decision rates by segment.
    4. Summarize simulated offer-exposure rates by segment.
    5. Summarize approved amount and approved Expected Loss by segment.
    6. Calculate approved Expected Loss rate by segment.
    7. Summarize offered-population synthetic estimated-risk proxy and approved PTI by segment.
    8. Identify the top simulated primary reason code within each segment.
    9. Display segment highlights ordered toward higher approved Expected Loss exposure.

INPUT:
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive

OUTPUT:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights

SEGMENT LEVELS:
    PRODUCT:
        One row per product_type_policy_key.

    SCORE_BAND:
        One row per score_band_policy_key.

    PRODUCT_X_SCORE:
        One row per product_type_policy_key × score_band_policy_key.

WHY SEGMENT-LEVEL FRONTIER HIGHLIGHTS MATTER:
    Portfolio-level frontier metrics can hide concentration.

    A strategy may look balanced at the portfolio level while most approved Expected Loss,
    counteroffer usage, or no-offer concentration is coming from one product × score intersection.

    Section 15.6 shows where the strategy story is actually concentrated.

CREDIT RISK INTERPRETATION:
    Product type affects:
        requested exposure,
        product caps,
        payment mechanics,
        LGD,
        counteroffer feasibility,
        approved amount,
        and approved Expected Loss.

    Score band affects:
        borrower-quality segmentation,
        synthetic estimated-risk profile,
        decision routing,
        offer-exposure rate,
        and policy-stop concentration.

    Product × score intersections are often the best place to explain frontier movement.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    total_approved_expected_loss_amount:
        Highest risk-impact segments.

    total_approved_amount:
        Highest exposure segments.

    simulated_offer_exposure_rate:
        Segment-level access / positive-offer concentration.

    avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows:
        Offered-population synthetic risk profile.

    approved_expected_loss_rate:
        Approved Expected Loss per approved exposure dollar.

    top_segment_reason_code:
        Most common simulated explanation code in the segment.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    High approved Expected Loss in a product × score segment:
        Review product, score band, LGD, approved amount, and reason-code distribution.

    High offer exposure in weaker score bands:
        Review approved Expected Loss and risk posture.

    High counteroffer rate:
        Review counteroffer feasibility, product caps, requested PTI, and approved Expected Loss cap.

    High decline rate:
        Review policy-stop concentration and whether the strategy posture explains the no-offer
        share.

METRIC CONVENTIONS:
    All-application approved metrics:
        Use COALESCE(approved_amount, 0)
        Use COALESCE(approved_expected_loss_amount, 0)

    Offer-row metrics:
        Use FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)

    approved_expected_loss_rate:
        total approved Expected Loss / total approved amount

    estimated_pd:
        SQL field name retained; interpreted as the synthetic estimated-PD proxy.

REASON-CODE BOUNDARY:
    top_segment_reason_code is an internal simulated strategy explanation code.

    It is not a customer communication.
    It is not an adverse-action reason.
    It is not a regulatory notice.
    It is not a fair-lending conclusion.

SQL CONCEPTS TAUGHT IN THIS SECTION:
    UNION ALL:
        Stacks PRODUCT, SCORE_BAND, and PRODUCT_X_SCORE segment views into a single segment_base CTE.

    GROUP BY:
        Collapses applicant-level archive rows into segment-level summaries.

    FILTER:
        Calculates offer-row and outcome-specific metrics without removing non-offer rows from the
        grouped population.

    COALESCE:
        Converts no-offer or unresolved approved values to zero when calculating all-application
        portfolio impact.

    ROW_NUMBER:
        Ranks reason codes within each segment so the top segment reason can be selected
        deterministically.

    LEFT JOIN:
        Adds the top reason code while preserving every segment summary row.

BOUNDARY:
    Section 15.6 is read-only against archive rows.

    It does not create final outcomes.
    It does not alter strategy outputs.
    It does not recalculate approved exposure.
    It does not recalculate approved Expected Loss.
    It does not reassign simulated reason codes.

***************************************************************************************************/

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights;

CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights
ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS
AS
WITH segment_base AS (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    PRODUCT-level segment rows.

    Business purpose:
        Summarize strategy frontier behavior by product.

    SQL mechanics:
        segment_level is set to PRODUCT.
        segment_key is the product key.
        score_band_policy_key is set to NULL because this level is product-only.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT
        'PRODUCT'::TEXT AS segment_level,
        product_type_policy_key AS segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        NULL::TEXT AS score_band_policy_key,

        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,

        decision_outcome,
        simulated_offer_exposure_flag,
        approved_amount,
        approved_expected_loss_amount,
        estimated_pd,
        approved_payment_to_income_ratio,
        primary_reason_family,
        primary_reason_code

    FROM credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive

    UNION ALL

    /***********************************************************************************************
    SCORE_BAND-level segment rows.

    Business purpose:
        Summarize strategy frontier behavior by borrower-quality segment.

    SQL mechanics:
        segment_level is set to SCORE_BAND.
        segment_key is the score-band key.
        product_type_policy_key is set to NULL because this level is score-only.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT
        'SCORE_BAND'::TEXT AS segment_level,
        score_band_policy_key AS segment_key,
        NULL::TEXT AS product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,

        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,

        decision_outcome,
        simulated_offer_exposure_flag,
        approved_amount,
        approved_expected_loss_amount,
        estimated_pd,
        approved_payment_to_income_ratio,
        primary_reason_family,
        primary_reason_code

    FROM credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive

    UNION ALL

    /***********************************************************************************************
    PRODUCT_X_SCORE-level segment rows.

    Business purpose:
        Summarize strategy frontier behavior at the product × borrower-quality intersection.

    SQL mechanics:
        segment_key is a readable concatenation of product and score-band keys.

    Interpretation:
        This is often the most important segment layer for explaining frontier movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT
        'PRODUCT_X_SCORE'::TEXT AS segment_level,
        product_type_policy_key || ' / ' || score_band_policy_key AS segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,

        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,

        decision_outcome,
        simulated_offer_exposure_flag,
        approved_amount,
        approved_expected_loss_amount,
        estimated_pd,
        approved_payment_to_income_ratio,
        primary_reason_family,
        primary_reason_code

    FROM credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive
),

segment_summary AS (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    CTE: segment_summary

    Purpose:
        Aggregate archive rows into segment-level frontier metrics.

    Grain:
        segment_level
        segment_key
        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

    Metric convention:
        Approved exposure and approved Expected Loss use all-application COALESCE-to-zero treatment
        for portfolio-impact metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT
        segment_level,
        segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,

        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Segment population.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) AS applications,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Final outcome rates by segment.

        SQL mechanics:
            FILTER creates the numerator for each outcome.
            COUNT(*) is the segment denominator.
            NULLIF protects against divide-by-zero.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE decision_outcome = 'APPROVE')::NUMERIC
            / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS approval_rate,

        COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE decision_outcome = 'COUNTEROFFER')::NUMERIC
            / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS counteroffer_rate,

        COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE decision_outcome = 'MANUAL_REVIEW')::NUMERIC
            / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS manual_review_rate,

        COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE decision_outcome = 'DECLINE')::NUMERIC
            / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS decline_rate,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Simulated positive offer-exposure rate.

        TRUE for APPROVE and COUNTEROFFER.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)::NUMERIC
            / NULLIF(COUNT(*), 0) AS simulated_offer_exposure_rate,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Segment approved exposure metrics.

        total_approved_amount:
            all-application approved exposure impact.

        avg_approved_amount_offer_rows:
            average approved amount only among simulated positive offer-exposure rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        SUM(COALESCE(approved_amount, 0)) AS total_approved_amount,

        AVG(approved_amount) FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)
            AS avg_approved_amount_offer_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Segment approved Expected Loss metrics.

        approved_expected_loss_rate:
            synthetic strategy metric equal to total approved Expected Loss divided by total
            approved amount.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        SUM(COALESCE(approved_expected_loss_amount, 0))
            AS total_approved_expected_loss_amount,

        AVG(approved_expected_loss_amount) FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)
            AS avg_approved_expected_loss_offer_rows,

        SUM(COALESCE(approved_expected_loss_amount, 0))
            / NULLIF(SUM(COALESCE(approved_amount, 0)), 0)
            AS approved_expected_loss_rate,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Offered-population synthetic risk and affordability profile.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        AVG(estimated_pd) FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)
            AS avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows,

        AVG(approved_payment_to_income_ratio) FILTER (WHERE simulated_offer_exposure_flag IS TRUE)
            AS avg_approved_payment_to_income_ratio_offer_rows

    FROM segment_base

    GROUP BY
        segment_level,
        segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,
        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id
),

segment_reason_counts AS (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    CTE: segment_reason_counts

    Purpose:
        Count and rank primary reason codes within each segment / strategy / scenario frontier row.

    SQL mechanics:
        COUNT(*) counts reason-code frequency.
        ROW_NUMBER ranks the highest-frequency reason code first.

    Deterministic tie-break:
        primary_reason_family, then primary_reason_code.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT
        segment_level,
        segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,

        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,

        primary_reason_family,
        primary_reason_code,

        COUNT(*) AS reason_row_count,

        ROW_NUMBER() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                segment_level,
                segment_key,
                strategy_set_name,
                strategy_name,
                strategy_version,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                COUNT(*) DESC,
                primary_reason_family,
                primary_reason_code
        ) AS reason_rank

    FROM segment_base

    GROUP BY
        segment_level,
        segment_key,
        product_type_policy_key,
        score_band_policy_key,
        strategy_set_name,
        strategy_name,
        strategy_version,
        module1_scenario_set_name,
        module1_scenario_name,
        population_id,
        primary_reason_family,
        primary_reason_code
)

SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Segment frontier metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    s.*,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Top simulated primary reason code for the segment.

    Interpretation:
        This helps explain the most common strategy explanation pattern within each segment.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.primary_reason_family AS top_segment_reason_family,
    r.primary_reason_code AS top_segment_reason_code,
    r.reason_row_count AS top_segment_reason_row_count,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Top reason-code share within the segment.

    Formula:
        top reason row count / segment applications
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.reason_row_count::NUMERIC / NULLIF(s.applications, 0)
        AS top_segment_reason_population_share

FROM segment_summary s

LEFT JOIN segment_reason_counts r
    ON r.segment_level = s.segment_level
   AND r.segment_key = s.segment_key
   AND r.strategy_set_name = s.strategy_set_name
   AND r.strategy_name = s.strategy_name
   AND r.strategy_version = s.strategy_version
   AND r.module1_scenario_set_name = s.module1_scenario_set_name
   AND r.module1_scenario_name = s.module1_scenario_name
   AND r.population_id = s.population_id
   AND r.reason_rank = 1;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15.6A Segment-level frontier highlights review output.

Business purpose:
    Display product, score-band, and product × score-band frontier segment summaries.

What to look for:
    Review rows with the highest total_approved_expected_loss_amount first.

    Those rows often drive the risk / exposure story.

    Also review:
        high simulated_offer_exposure_rate,
        high approved_expected_loss_rate,
        high counteroffer_rate,
        high decline_rate,
        high top_segment_reason_population_share.

Review triggers:
    A product × score segment with high approved Expected Loss should be interpreted before
    executive storytelling.

    A segment with high approved exposure but weak score-band profile should be explained through
    strategy posture and reason-code drivers.

SQL mechanics:
    ORDER BY prioritizes higher approved Expected Loss segments within each strategy / scenario /
    segment level.

    LIMIT 300 is a review ergonomics limit only. It does not change the temporary table contents.

Documentation use:
    Strong companion table for executive summary and validation evidence because it identifies where
    portfolio-level frontier movement is concentrated.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

SELECT
    *
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights
ORDER BY
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,
    strategy_set_name,
    strategy_name,
    strategy_version,
    segment_level,
    total_approved_expected_loss_amount DESC NULLS LAST,
    segment_key
LIMIT 300;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.7 — EXECUTIVE-READY STRATEGY FRONTIER SUMMARY
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Produce a compact strategy frontier table suitable for export into README, executive review,
    LinkedIn result summary, future presentation artifacts, Section 16 evidence capture, or
    validation-summary planning.

    Section 15.7 answers:

        "What is the concise executive interpretation of each archived strategy frontier point?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Drop any prior tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary.
    2. Create tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary.
    3. Rank strategies within each comparable frontier group.
    4. Carry core frontier metrics.
    5. Carry baseline-relative deltas.
    6. Carry reason-code story fields.
    7. Carry reason-code movement fields.
    8. Enrich frontier rows with campaign registry IDs where available.
    9. Assign executive_frontier_quadrant.
    10. Display the executive-ready frontier output.

INPUTS:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map

OUTPUT:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary

EXECUTIVE SUMMARY GRAIN:
    One row per archived:

        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

REGISTRY ENRICHMENT:
    Section 15.7 LEFT JOINs frontier rows to:

        tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map

    using the technical run key:

        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id
        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version

REGISTRY FIELDS CARRIED:
    frontier_registry_match_status
    frontier_registry_match_count
    module1_run_id
    module2_run_id
    run_sequence_number
    showcase_id
    showcase_name
    comparison_group_id
    validation_evidence_group_id
    readme_storyline_label
    linkedin_post_sequence
    validation_summary_section_hint

REGISTRY INTERPRETATION:
    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_MATCHED:
        The archived frontier row has exactly one active campaign registry mapping.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_MATCH_REVIEW:
        More than one active registry row exists for the same technical key.

        Section 15 selected one ranked mapping to avoid duplicate frontier rows, but the registry
        should be reviewed.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND:
        No active registry row exists for this archived frontier point.

        The frontier metrics remain valid, but documentation linkage is incomplete.

BUSINESS INTERPRETATION:
    This table intentionally combines:
        decision distribution,
        simulated offer exposure,
        approved exposure,
        approved Expected Loss,
        synthetic efficiency metrics,
        strategy posture classification,
        reason-code concentration,
        reason-code movement,
        frontier ranks,
        executive quadrant labels,
        and campaign registry identifiers.

    It is designed to support a concise story:

        "What tradeoff does each strategy / scenario point represent?"

RANKING BOUNDARY:
    Rankings are calculated within each:

        strategy_set_name
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

    These ranks support comparison.

    They do not declare production suitability.
    They do not identify a single approved strategy.
    They do not replace Section 13 matched comparison.
    They do not replace Section 14 validation.

EXECUTIVE FRONTIER QUADRANT BOUNDARY:
    executive_frontier_quadrant is a diagnostic storytelling label.

    It is not:
        a production recommendation,
        a risk appetite approval,
        a validation acceptance decision,
        a profitability conclusion,
        or a model governance signoff.

QUADRANT INTERPRETATION:
    BASELINE_REFERENCE_QUADRANT:
        Baseline reference point.

    HIGHER_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE:
        Strategy improved the access / approved Expected Loss rate tradeoff.

    HIGHER_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE:
        Strategy expanded access with a higher approved Expected Loss rate tradeoff.

    LOWER_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE:
        Strategy reduced access but lowered approved Expected Loss rate.

    LOWER_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE:
        Strategy reduced access and worsened approved Expected Loss rate, requiring review.

    TWO_STRATEGY_MIXED_FRONTIER_REVIEW:
        Two-strategy comparison does not fit a clean quadrant.

SQL CONCEPTS TAUGHT IN THIS SECTION:
    CTE ranked:
        Adds rank columns before the final registry enrichment and quadrant assignment.

    COUNT(*) OVER:
        Counts how many strategies exist in each comparable frontier group.

    RANK:
        Ranks strategies within each group by access, approved amount, approved Expected Loss,
        approved Expected Loss rate, offered-population synthetic risk, manual-review burden, and
        decline burden.

    LEFT JOIN:
        Adds optional registry metadata without dropping frontier rows that lack registry mapping.

    COALESCE:
        Converts missing registry status into FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND.

    CASE:
        Assigns executive_frontier_quadrant using baseline-relative logic for two-strategy groups
        and rank-based logic for groups with three or more strategies.

READ-ONLY BOUNDARY:
    Section 15.7 creates a temporary executive summary only.

    It does not modify archive rows.
    It does not modify latest-run rows.
    It does not modify registry rows.
    It does not insert evidence rows.
    It does not recalculate strategy outputs.
    It does not reassign reason codes.

***************************************************************************************************/

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary;

CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary
ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS
AS
WITH ranked AS (
    SELECT
        /*******************************************************************************************
        Carry all fields from Section 15.5 classification.

        This includes:
            frontier metrics,
            baseline deltas,
            reason-code fields,
            efficiency metrics,
            classification labels.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        c.*,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Count strategies in the comparable frontier group.

        Group grain:
            strategy_set_name
            module1_scenario_set_name
            module1_scenario_name
            population_id

        Interpretation:
            Fewer than three strategies limits rank-based quadrant interpretation.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
        ) AS strategy_count_within_frontier_group,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Access rank.

        Rank 1 = highest simulated offer-exposure rate.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                simulated_offer_exposure_rate DESC NULLS LAST
        ) AS offer_access_rank_desc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Approved amount rank.

        Rank 1 = highest total approved amount.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                total_approved_amount DESC NULLS LAST
        ) AS approved_amount_rank_desc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Total approved Expected Loss rank.

        Rank 1 = lowest total approved Expected Loss.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                total_approved_expected_loss_amount ASC NULLS LAST
        ) AS total_approved_expected_loss_rank_asc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Approved Expected Loss rate rank.

        Rank 1 = lowest approved Expected Loss rate.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                approved_expected_loss_rate ASC NULLS LAST
        ) AS approved_expected_loss_rate_rank_asc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Offered-population synthetic risk rank.

        Rank 1 = lowest average synthetic estimated-PD proxy among simulated offer rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows ASC NULLS LAST
        ) AS offer_risk_profile_rank_asc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Manual-review burden rank.

        Rank 1 = lowest manual-review rate.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                manual_review_rate ASC NULLS LAST
        ) AS manual_review_burden_rank_asc,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Decline burden rank.

        Rank 1 = lowest decline rate.

        Interpretation:
            Lower decline burden can support access, but must be interpreted alongside approved
            Expected Loss and offered-population risk.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        RANK() OVER (
            PARTITION BY
                strategy_set_name,
                module1_scenario_set_name,
                module1_scenario_name,
                population_id
            ORDER BY
                decline_rate ASC NULLS LAST
        ) AS decline_burden_rank_asc

    FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification c
)

SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Registry linkage fields.

    SQL mechanics:
        COALESCE labels missing registry matches as FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND.

    Interpretation:
        Missing registry mapping does not invalidate frontier metrics.
        It means formal documentation linkage should be reviewed.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(reg.frontier_registry_match_status, 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND')
        AS frontier_registry_match_status,

    COALESCE(reg.registry_match_count, 0)
        AS frontier_registry_match_count,

    reg.module1_run_id,
    reg.module2_run_id,
    reg.run_sequence_number,

    reg.showcase_id,
    reg.showcase_name,
    reg.comparison_group_id,
    reg.validation_evidence_group_id,

    reg.readme_storyline_label,
    reg.linkedin_post_sequence,
    reg.validation_summary_section_hint,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 1 scenario / population lineage.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.module1_scenario_set_name,
    r.module1_scenario_name,
    r.population_id,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 2 strategy identity.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.strategy_set_name,
    r.strategy_name,
    r.strategy_version,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Frontier posture classification.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.frontier_position_type,
    r.frontier_interpretation_note,

    r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Portfolio size and final outcome rates.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.applications,

    r.approval_rate,
    r.counteroffer_rate,
    r.manual_review_rate,
    r.decline_rate,
    r.simulated_offer_exposure_rate,
    r.counteroffer_share_of_offers,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Approved exposure, offered-population risk, affordability, and approved Expected Loss metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.total_approved_amount,
    r.avg_approved_amount_offer_rows,

    r.avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows,
    r.avg_approved_payment_to_income_ratio_offer_rows,

    r.total_approved_expected_loss_amount,
    r.avg_approved_expected_loss_offer_rows,
    r.approved_expected_loss_rate,

    r.approved_amount_per_approved_expected_loss_dollar,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Reason-code story fields.

    Boundary:
        These are simulated strategy explanation codes only.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.top_primary_reason_family,
    r.top_primary_reason_code,
    r.top_primary_reason_population_share,

    r.top_non_approval_primary_reason_family,
    r.top_non_approval_primary_reason_code,
    r.top_non_approval_reason_population_share,

    r.top_policy_stop_primary_reason_family,
    r.top_policy_stop_primary_reason_code,
    r.top_policy_stop_reason_population_share,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Largest reason-code movement versus baseline.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.top_reason_code_delta_driver_family,
    r.top_reason_code_delta_driver_code,
    r.top_reason_code_delta_driver_baseline_share,
    r.top_reason_code_delta_driver_strategy_share,
    r.top_reason_code_delta_population_share,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Baseline-relative availability and movement.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.baseline_available_flag,

    r.delta_approval_rate,
    r.delta_counteroffer_rate,
    r.delta_manual_review_rate,
    r.delta_decline_rate,
    r.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate,

    r.delta_total_approved_amount,
    r.delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount,
    r.delta_approved_expected_loss_rate,
    r.delta_avg_synthetic_estimated_pd_proxy_offer_rows,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Frontier ranks within comparable group.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    r.offer_access_rank_desc,
    r.approved_amount_rank_desc,
    r.total_approved_expected_loss_rank_asc,
    r.approved_expected_loss_rate_rank_asc,
    r.offer_risk_profile_rank_asc,
    r.manual_review_burden_rank_asc,
    r.decline_burden_rank_asc,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Executive frontier quadrant.

    SQL mechanics:
        Two-strategy groups use baseline-relative delta signs because rank-based interpretation is
        thin with only two points.

        Three-or-more strategy groups use rank-based quadrant logic.

    Boundary:
        Diagnostic storytelling label only.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    CASE
        /*******************************************************************************************
        Baseline row.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN r.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
            THEN 'BASELINE_REFERENCE_QUADRANT'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Two-strategy frontier groups.

        These use baseline-relative movement because rank positions alone are not enough to explain
        access / approved EL rate direction.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group < 3
         AND r.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate < 0
         AND r.delta_approved_expected_loss_rate < 0
            THEN 'LOWER_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group < 3
         AND r.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate > 0
         AND r.delta_approved_expected_loss_rate > 0
            THEN 'HIGHER_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group < 3
         AND r.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate >= 0
         AND r.delta_approved_expected_loss_rate <= 0
            THEN 'HIGHER_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group < 3
         AND r.delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate <= 0
         AND r.delta_approved_expected_loss_rate >= 0
            THEN 'LOWER_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.strategy_count_within_frontier_group < 3
            THEN 'TWO_STRATEGY_MIXED_FRONTIER_REVIEW'

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Three-or-more strategy frontier groups.

        These use rank positions:
            high access = rank <= 2 on offer access.
            lower approved EL rate = rank <= 2 on approved EL rate.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        WHEN r.offer_access_rank_desc <= 2
         AND r.approved_expected_loss_rate_rank_asc <= 2
            THEN 'HIGH_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.offer_access_rank_desc <= 2
         AND r.approved_expected_loss_rate_rank_asc > 2
            THEN 'HIGH_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        WHEN r.offer_access_rank_desc > 2
         AND r.approved_expected_loss_rate_rank_asc <= 2
            THEN 'LOWER_ACCESS_LOWER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'

        ELSE 'LOWER_ACCESS_HIGHER_APPROVED_EL_RATE'
    END AS executive_frontier_quadrant

FROM ranked r

LEFT JOIN tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map reg
    ON reg.strategy_set_name = r.strategy_set_name
   AND reg.strategy_name = r.strategy_name
   AND reg.strategy_version = r.strategy_version
   AND reg.module1_scenario_set_name = r.module1_scenario_set_name
   AND reg.module1_scenario_name = r.module1_scenario_name
   AND reg.population_id = r.population_id;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
15.7A Executive-ready strategy frontier summary review output.

Business purpose:
    Display the primary Section 15 output for high-level strategy storytelling.

What to look for:
    frontier_registry_match_status:
        confirms whether the row is linked to formal campaign registry IDs.

    executive_frontier_quadrant:
        broad access / approved Expected Loss rate position.

    frontier_position_type:
        diagnostic strategy posture.

    delta_simulated_offer_exposure_rate:
        baseline-relative access movement.

    delta_total_approved_expected_loss_amount:
        baseline-relative approved Expected Loss movement.

    top_reason_code_delta_driver_code:
        strongest reason-code movement versus baseline.

Review triggers:
    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND:
        frontier metrics are valid, but documentation traceability is incomplete.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_MATCH_REVIEW:
        registry mapping should be cleaned before formal documentation.

    TWO_STRATEGY_MIXED_FRONTIER_REVIEW:
        use segment and reason-code diagnostics before executive narrative.

SQL mechanics:
    This SELECT reads the temporary executive summary table.

    It does not re-run the ranking logic.

Documentation use:
    This is the primary export-ready frontier summary table.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

SELECT
    *
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary
ORDER BY
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,
    strategy_set_name,
    CASE
        WHEN strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY' THEN 1
        ELSE 2
    END,
    frontier_position_type,
    strategy_name,
    strategy_version;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.8 — FRONTIER DIAGNOSTIC SUMMARY
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Provide compact diagnostic outputs for the Section 15 temporary frontier layer.

    Section 15.8 answers:

        "Did the Section 15 frontier layer create the expected temporary objects, and are baseline,
         reason-code, and registry-linkage fields interpretable?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Count rows in each Section 15 temporary frontier object.
    2. Show baseline-reference availability.
    3. Show compact reason-code storytelling fields for each executive frontier point.
    4. Show registry-linkage diagnostics for the executive frontier table.

INTERPRETATION:
    These checks are not full QA gatekeepers.

    They confirm that the frontier layer produced populated temporary outputs and show whether:
        baseline-relative comparisons are available,
        reason-code storytelling fields are available,
        registry IDs are available,
        and documentation linkage is clean.

    Full latest-run QA remains Section 14.

    Archive persistence validation remains Section 12.

    Matched baseline / challenger comparison remains Section 13.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    Frontier object row counts:
        most objects should contain rows when the archive contains frontier slices.

    Baseline availability:
        confirms whether baseline-relative deltas are interpretable.

    Reason-code storytelling:
        confirms whether executive reason-code fields can support narrative.

    Registry linkage:
        confirms whether the frontier output can be tied to stable run IDs.

BOUNDARY:
    These diagnostics are read-only.

    They do not modify frontier objects.
    They do not modify archive rows.
    They do not modify latest-run rows.
    They do not modify campaign registry rows.
    They do not recalculate decisions.
    They do not recalculate approved exposure.
    They do not recalculate approved Expected Loss.
    They do not reassign simulated reason codes.

***************************************************************************************************/



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.8A — FRONTIER OBJECT ROW-COUNT DIAGNOSTIC
****************************************************************************************************

PURPOSE:
    Confirm that each Section 15 temporary frontier object exists and contains rows.

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Give reviewers a compact health check for the Section 15 temporary frontier layer.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_inventory:
        should have one row per archived strategy / scenario / population slice.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_summary:
        should align to inventory slice count.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_baseline_deltas:
        should align to frontier summary row count.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency:
        should align to baseline-delta row count.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification:
        should align to efficiency row count.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary:
        should align to classification row count.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights:
        will usually have more rows because it includes product, score-band, and product × score
        segment grains.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map:
        may be zero if registry metadata is unavailable or no active registry rows exist.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    Zero row count in core frontier objects:
        archive may be empty or an upstream Section 15 object did not run.

    Zero row count in registry map:
        not a metric failure, but campaign-ID linkage may be unavailable.

SQL MECHANICS:
    UNION ALL stacks one count per temporary object.

BOUNDARY:
    This diagnostic only counts temporary output rows.

***************************************************************************************************/

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_campaign_registry_map

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_inventory' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_inventory

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_summary' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_summary

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_baseline_deltas' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_baseline_deltas

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_efficiency

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_classification

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_segment_highlights

UNION ALL

SELECT
    'tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary' AS frontier_object_name,
    COUNT(*) AS row_count
FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.8B — FRONTIER BASELINE AVAILABILITY DIAGNOSTIC
****************************************************************************************************

PURPOSE:
    Confirm whether archived frontier rows have baseline references available.

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Baseline-relative deltas are most useful when every strategy frontier row has a matching
    BASELINE_STRATEGY under the same Module 1 scenario / population / strategy set.

CREDIT RISK INTERPRETATION:
    A pure Module 2 strategy frontier should compare multiple Module 2 strategies while holding the
    Module 1 scenario and population constant.

    Baseline availability allows strategy reviewers to say:

        "Relative to the baseline strategy on the same synthetic population, this strategy changed
         access, approved exposure, approved Expected Loss, and reason-code mix by X."

    Without baseline availability, the archive row remains useful as an absolute summary but not as a
    baseline-relative strategy movement point.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    rows_without_baseline_reference:
        should ideally be zero for formal strategy-frontier campaigns.

    baseline_reference_status:
        should ideally be BASELINE_REFERENCES_AVAILABLE_FOR_ALL_FRONTIER_ROWS.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    BASELINE_REFERENCE_MISSING_FOR_ONE_OR_MORE_FRONTIER_ROWS:
        Do not overstate baseline-relative movement for those rows.

    rows_without_baseline_reference > 0:
        determine whether the baseline strategy slice should be archived.

SQL MECHANICS:
    GROUP BY:
        summarizes baseline availability at the comparable frontier group grain.

    COUNT(*) FILTER:
        counts rows with and without baseline references in the same grouped output.

    CASE:
        converts the count pattern into a readable status.

BOUNDARY:
    Missing baseline reference is not an archive failure by itself.

    It is a comparison-readiness and storytelling limitation.

***************************************************************************************************/

SELECT
    strategy_set_name,
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Number of frontier rows in this comparable group.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COUNT(*) AS frontier_rows,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Count rows where baseline-relative deltas are available.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE baseline_available_flag IS TRUE)
        AS rows_with_baseline_reference,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Count rows where baseline-relative deltas are not available.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE baseline_available_flag IS FALSE)
        AS rows_without_baseline_reference,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Readable baseline-reference status.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    CASE
        WHEN COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE baseline_available_flag IS FALSE) = 0
            THEN 'BASELINE_REFERENCES_AVAILABLE_FOR_ALL_FRONTIER_ROWS'
        ELSE 'BASELINE_REFERENCE_MISSING_FOR_ONE_OR_MORE_FRONTIER_ROWS'
    END AS baseline_reference_status

FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_baseline_deltas

GROUP BY
    strategy_set_name,
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id

ORDER BY
    strategy_set_name,
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.8C — FRONTIER REASON-CODE STORYTELLING DIAGNOSTIC
****************************************************************************************************

PURPOSE:
    Provide a compact reason-code interpretation view for the strategy frontier summary.

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Help explain each archived strategy frontier point by highlighting:
        top primary reason code overall,
        top non-approval reason code,
        top policy-stop reason code,
        and the reason code with the largest population-share movement versus baseline.

CREDIT RISK INTERPRETATION:
    Numeric deltas explain how much a strategy changed.

    Reason-code movement helps explain why the strategy changed.

    For example:
        higher policy-stop reason share may explain lower simulated offer exposure;
        higher counteroffer reason share may explain increased reduced-exposure treatment;
        higher manual-review reason share may explain unresolved burden;
        higher approval reason share may explain expanded access.

WHY NON-APPROVAL REASON FIELDS MATTER:
    top_primary_reason_code is often APPROVED_AS_REQUESTED.

    That is correct, but it is not always the most useful explanation of strategy movement.

    top_non_approval_primary_reason_code helps identify the most common constraint among rows that
    did not receive clean approval.

WHY POLICY-STOP REASON FIELDS MATTER:
    top_policy_stop_primary_reason_code helps explain no-offer concentration and risk-tightening
    effects.

REGISTRY PATCH:
    This output includes:
        module1_run_id,
        module2_run_id,
        run_sequence_number,
        showcase_id,
        comparison_group_id,
        validation_evidence_group_id

    where available, so the reason-code story can be copied directly into campaign documentation.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_code:
        strongest explanation-code movement versus baseline.

    top_reason_code_delta_population_share:
        direction and size of the reason-code population-share shift.

    top_non_approval_primary_reason_code:
        strongest non-approval explanation.

    top_policy_stop_primary_reason_code:
        strongest no-offer explanation.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    Large reason-code movement should be connected to strategy posture.

    Missing registry IDs should be interpreted as documentation-linkage gaps, not metric failures.

BOUNDARY:
    This is a read-only storytelling diagnostic.

    It does not recalculate decision outcomes.
    It does not reassign simulated reason codes.
    It does not modify latest-run rows.
    It does not modify archive rows.
    It does not modify campaign registry rows.

    Reason codes remain simulated strategy explanation codes only.

***************************************************************************************************/

SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Registry and documentation linkage.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    frontier_registry_match_status,
    module1_run_id,
    module2_run_id,
    run_sequence_number,
    showcase_id,
    showcase_name,
    comparison_group_id,
    validation_evidence_group_id,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Strategy posture fields.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    strategy_name,
    frontier_position_type,
    executive_frontier_quadrant,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Top primary reason code overall.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    top_primary_reason_code,
    top_primary_reason_population_share,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Top non-approval reason code.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    top_non_approval_primary_reason_code,
    top_non_approval_reason_population_share,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Top policy-stop reason code.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    top_policy_stop_primary_reason_code,
    top_policy_stop_reason_population_share,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Largest reason-code movement versus baseline.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_code,
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_baseline_share,
    top_reason_code_delta_driver_strategy_share,
    top_reason_code_delta_population_share

FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary

ORDER BY
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,
    strategy_set_name,
    CASE
        WHEN strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY' THEN 1
        ELSE 2
    END,
    strategy_name;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 15.8D — FRONTIER REGISTRY-LINKAGE DIAGNOSTIC
****************************************************************************************************

PURPOSE:
    Summarize whether executive frontier rows are linked to the campaign registry.

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Confirm whether the archive-wide frontier output can be referenced through stable campaign IDs.

    This matters before:
        exporting the frontier summary,
        drafting README results,
        preparing LinkedIn post evidence,
        selecting rows for future Validation Summary,
        or using the frontier output as a campaign evidence source.

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Count executive frontier rows.
    2. Count rows with clean registry matches.
    3. Count rows missing registry matches.
    4. Count rows with duplicate registry match review status.
    5. Count rows missing module2_run_id.
    6. Count rows missing validation_evidence_group_id.
    7. Assign registry_linkage_status.

STATUS VALUES:
    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_READY:
        Every executive frontier row has a clean registry match.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_PARTIAL:
        Some rows have registry mappings and some do not.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_NOT_AVAILABLE:
        No executive frontier rows have registry mappings.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_REVIEW_REQUIRED:
        At least one frontier row matched duplicate active registry mappings.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_REVIEW_REQUIRED:
        Fallback status for unexpected count combinations.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR:
    registry_matched_rows:
        should ideally equal executive_frontier_rows for formal campaign evidence.

    registry_missing_rows:
        should ideally be zero.

    registry_duplicate_review_rows:
        should be zero.

    missing_module2_run_id_rows:
        should be zero for documentation-ready outputs.

    missing_validation_evidence_group_id_rows:
        should be zero for validation-summary-ready outputs.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_REVIEW_REQUIRED:
        Clean registry mapping before documentation.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_PARTIAL:
        Identify which archived frontier points do not have formal run IDs.

    FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_NOT_AVAILABLE:
        Frontier metrics are available, but documentation traceability is incomplete.

SQL MECHANICS:
    registry_summary:
        collapses executive frontier output into one compact registry-linkage row.

    COUNT(*) FILTER:
        counts specific registry-match statuses and missing-ID conditions.

    CASE:
        converts row counts into a readable registry_linkage_status.

BOUNDARY:
    Missing registry linkage does not invalidate strategy frontier metrics.

    It limits documentation traceability.

    This query does not insert, update, or delete registry rows.

***************************************************************************************************/

WITH registry_summary AS (
    SELECT
        /*******************************************************************************************
        Total executive frontier rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) AS executive_frontier_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Clean one-to-one registry matches.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (
            WHERE frontier_registry_match_status = 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_MATCHED'
        ) AS registry_matched_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Missing registry matches.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (
            WHERE frontier_registry_match_status = 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND'
        ) AS registry_missing_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Duplicate active registry mapping review rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (
            WHERE frontier_registry_match_status = 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_MATCH_REVIEW'
        ) AS registry_duplicate_review_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Missing Module 2 run ID rows.

        Expected:
            zero for documentation-ready campaign rows.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (
            WHERE module2_run_id IS NULL
        ) AS missing_module2_run_id_rows,

        /*******************************************************************************************
        Missing validation evidence group ID rows.

        Expected:
            zero for Validation Summary traceability.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        COUNT(*) FILTER (
            WHERE validation_evidence_group_id IS NULL
        ) AS missing_validation_evidence_group_id_rows

    FROM tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary
)

SELECT
    executive_frontier_rows,
    registry_matched_rows,
    registry_missing_rows,
    registry_duplicate_review_rows,
    missing_module2_run_id_rows,
    missing_validation_evidence_group_id_rows,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Registry linkage status.

    Order matters:
        duplicate mappings are checked first because they are the highest-governance registry issue.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    CASE
        WHEN registry_duplicate_review_rows > 0
            THEN 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_DUPLICATE_REVIEW_REQUIRED'

        WHEN executive_frontier_rows > 0
         AND registry_matched_rows = executive_frontier_rows
            THEN 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_READY'

        WHEN registry_matched_rows > 0
         AND registry_missing_rows > 0
            THEN 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_PARTIAL'

        WHEN executive_frontier_rows > 0
         AND registry_matched_rows = 0
            THEN 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_NOT_AVAILABLE'

        ELSE 'FRONTIER_REGISTRY_LINKAGE_REVIEW_REQUIRED'
    END AS registry_linkage_status

FROM registry_summary;


/***************************************************************************************************
END OF SECTION 15 — STRATEGY FRONTIER SUMMARY AND EXECUTIVE COMPARISON VIEW

If execution reaches this point:
    1. Archive-backed frontier scope has been reviewed.
    2. Campaign registry mapping has been created or reviewed where registry rows exist.
    3. Every archived strategy / scenario / population slice has been summarized at frontier grain.
    4. Baseline-relative deltas have been calculated where same-scenario BASELINE_STRATEGY rows exist.
    5. Efficiency indicators have been calculated.
    6. Frontier posture classifications have been assigned.
    7. Product, score-band, and product × score segment highlights have been created.
    8. tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary is available as the primary export-ready frontier
       output.

Primary Section 15 output:
    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary

Section 16 dependency:
    Section 16.6 uses tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary to carry frontier fields into the
    persistent one-row executive evidence summary for the current run.

Section 16.9 dependency:
    Section 16.9 uses a fresh tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary to synchronize Section
    15-derived frontier fields into persistent executive evidence after the full campaign archive is
    complete.

Workflow guidance:
    For a single run:
        Run Section 16 after Section 15 if current-run evidence capture is needed.

    For a full campaign:
        After all planned runs have completed through Section 16.8, rerun Section 15 once from the
        completed archive, then run Section 16.9, then run Section 17.

Read-only boundary:
    Section 15 did not modify latest-run rows.
    Section 15 did not modify archive rows.
    Section 15 did not modify campaign registry rows.
    Section 15 did not modify persistent evidence rows.
    Section 15 did not modify strategy parameter rows.
    Section 15 did not modify product-policy parameter rows.
    Section 15 did not recalculate decision outcomes.
    Section 15 did not recalculate approved exposure values.
    Section 15 did not recalculate approved Expected Loss values.
    Section 15 did not reassign simulated reason codes.
    Section 15 did not implement revenue, earnings, pricing, funding-cost, profitability, capital, or
    risk-adjusted margin logic.

Next:
    For current-run evidence capture:
        Run Section 16.

    For post-campaign evidence synchronization:
        Run Section 16.9 after a fresh Section 15 run.

    For final campaign acceptance:
        Run Section 17 after Section 16.9.

***************************************************************************************************/


/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 16 — RUN-LEVEL STRATEGY EVIDENCE CAPTURE AND EXECUTIVE STORY REGISTER
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Persist compact, executive-ready evidence for each completed Module 2 strategy run.

    Section 16 answers:

        "For the current completed Module 2 latest-run strategy slice, what evidence should be
         captured so the result can be reviewed, validated, explained, compared, documented, and
         later used in portfolio storytelling?"

    The Module 2 latest-run table and archive table preserve applicant-level simulated strategy
    outputs.

    Section 13 produces matched comparison diagnostics.
    Section 14 produces latest-run validation status evidence.
    Section 15 produces archive-backed frontier evidence.

    Section 16 distills those outputs into persistent evidence tables so the project can support:
        repeatable campaign tracking,
        executive storytelling,
        validation-summary drafting,
        README / portfolio evidence,
        LinkedIn campaign evidence,
        parameter traceability,
        product × score segment traceability,
        and future Module 3 growth / economics design.

IMPORTANT SECTION 16 SCOPE CLARIFICATION:
    Section 16.0–16.8 is run-level evidence capture.

    Section 16.9 is post-campaign frontier evidence synchronization.

    These are related, but they serve different workflow moments.

        Section 16.0–16.8:
            Run after each completed strategy run.

        Section 16.9:
            Run only after the full campaign archive is complete and Section 15 has been rerun from
            the completed archive.

WHY THIS DISTINCTION MATTERS:
    Section 15 is archive-wide.

    Section 16.0–16.8 captures evidence one current run at a time.

    During a full campaign, early evidence rows may be captured before every baseline / challenger
    archive slice exists. After later runs or reruns are completed, Section 15 may produce updated
    frontier labels and baseline-relative reason-code movement fields.

    Section 16.9 refreshes those Section 15-derived frontier fields in persistent executive evidence
    after the completed campaign frontier is available.

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Identify the current completed latest-run Module 2 strategy slice.
    2. Consume the Section 3 campaign registry match as the preferred run-ID and story-ID source.
    3. Create fallback current-run story metadata only when registry fallback is allowed.
    4. Confirm required Section 14 and Section 15 temporary evidence outputs exist.
    5. Confirm strategy and product-policy parameter tables exist for parameter snapshot capture.
    6. Create persistent evidence tables if they do not already exist.
    7. Add registry-ID columns to existing evidence tables when needed.
    8. Delete prior evidence rows for the current run key only.
    9. Persist Section 14 validation-status evidence for the current run.
    10. Persist the exact active Module 2 strategy parameter card, product-policy card, and run-story
        metadata as JSONB.
    11. Persist current product × score segment evidence.
    12. Persist the current one-row executive evidence summary.
    13. Validate current-run evidence capture through local Section 16.7 gatekeepers.
    14. Display compact export-ready Section 16.8 diagnostics.

PRIMARY INPUTS:
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions
        Current completed latest-run Module 2 strategy output.

    tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        Temporary Section 3 campaign registry match helper.

        Preferred source for:
            module1_run_id
            module2_run_id
            run_sequence_number
            showcase_id
            showcase_name
            comparison_group_id
            validation_evidence_group_id
            readme_storyline_label
            linkedin_post_sequence
            validation_summary_section_hint
            run_registry_match_status

    tmp_m2_validation_status_summary
        Temporary Section 14 validation status summary.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary
        Temporary Section 15 strategy frontier executive summary.

        Section 16.6 reads this table to carry Section 15-derived frontier posture fields into the
        current one-row executive evidence summary.

    tmp_module2_strategy_params
        Temporary active Module 2 strategy parameter card.

    tmp_module2_product_policy_params
        Temporary active product-policy parameter card.

TEMPORARY OUTPUTS CREATED:
    tmp_m2_current_run_key
    tmp_m2_current_run_story_params

PERSISTENT OUTPUT TABLES CREATED OR REUSED:
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_evidence_summary
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_validation_evidence
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_parameter_snapshot
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_segment_evidence_summary

EVIDENCE GRAIN:
    The primary run-level evidence grain is:

        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

    This is the same practical completed-run grain used for archive replacement and strategy evidence
    interpretation.

CAMPAIGN REGISTRY ID ROLE:
    Campaign IDs are documentation and evidence-linkage metadata.

    They help connect:

        run roadmap
        → SQL output
        → archive slice
        → comparison result
        → executive evidence row
        → validation evidence rows
        → parameter snapshot
        → segment evidence
        → future Validation Summary claim
        → README / LinkedIn campaign storyline

    They do not replace the technical SQL run key.

    Technical run key fields remain:

        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id
        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version

WHY SECTION 16 EXISTS:
    During a multi-run scenario / strategy campaign, the user should not have to manually collect key
    results from many separate QA grids after every run.

    Section 16 captures the story in one governed evidence layer:
        What Module 1 scenario was used?
        What Module 2 strategy was applied?
        What campaign run IDs identify this run?
        What showcase and comparison group does this run support?
        What validation evidence group should this run feed?
        What strategy family changed?
        What was expected to happen?
        What were the final simulated outcomes?
        What was the simulated offer-exposure rate?
        What was the approved exposure?
        What was the approved Expected Loss?
        What product and score segments drove the result?
        Which simulated reason codes explain the result?
        Which validation checks passed?
        Which validation checks require interpretation?
        Which validation checks were not applicable?
        What parameter card produced the output?
        What evidence will support later Module 3 growth / economics design?

WHY SECTION 16 USES EVIDENCE TABLES INSTEAD OF THE LATEST-RUN TABLE:
    credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions is replaceable.

    It represents only the current completed latest-run output.

    Evidence tables persist the executive story, validation summary, parameter snapshot, and segment
    profile for completed runs across the campaign.

WHY SECTION 16 DOES NOT WRITE TO THE ARCHIVE:
    Archive persistence is governed by Section 12.

    Section 16 does not insert, delete, update, or repair archive rows.

    Evidence capture and archive persistence are separate governance functions.

WHY DELETE / INSERT IS USED FOR CURRENT-RUN EVIDENCE:
    A strategy slice may be rerun under governed archive replacement behavior.

    Evidence rows should reflect the current accepted version of that run key, not accumulate stale
    duplicates for the same strategy / scenario / population evidence grain.

    Therefore Section 16 deletes and replaces evidence rows only for the current run key in the
    evidence tables.

WHY PARAMETER SNAPSHOTS ARE CAPTURED:
    The active strategy and product-policy tables are temporary.

    Without a persistent parameter snapshot, later documentation may show what happened but not
    exactly which thresholds, product caps, counteroffer controls, archive controls, and registry IDs
    produced the run.

    Section 16.4 snapshots:
        active strategy parameters,
        product-policy parameters,
        run-story / registry metadata,
        and a parameter snapshot hash.

WHY PRODUCT × SCORE SEGMENT EVIDENCE IS CAPTURED:
    The one-row executive evidence summary is intentionally compact.

    Product × score segment evidence preserves the drill-down layer needed to support executive
    interpretation.

    Strategy movement often concentrates in segments such as:
        unsecured / weaker-score applicants,
        revolving / weaker-score applicants,
        secured / stronger-score applicants,
        near-prime product segments,
        or high-balance product segments.

WHY FRONTIER FIELDS COME FROM SECTION 15:
    Section 15 already calculates archive-backed frontier posture, executive quadrant, and
    baseline-relative reason-code movement.

    Section 16.6 carries those fields into persistent evidence rather than recalculating the frontier.

    After the full campaign is complete, Section 16.9 can refresh those fields from a fresh Section
    15 run.

READ / WRITE BOUNDARY:
    Section 16.0–16.8 may create persistent evidence tables.

    Section 16.0–16.8 may delete and replace evidence rows for the current run key only in:

        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_evidence_summary
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_validation_evidence
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_parameter_snapshot
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_segment_evidence_summary

    Section 16.0–16.8 does not modify:
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_campaign_registry
        credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_comparison_campaign_registry

MODELING BOUNDARY:
    Section 16 does not recalculate decision_outcome.
    Section 16 does not recalculate decision_path.
    Section 16 does not recalculate approved_amount.
    Section 16 does not recalculate approved_payment_to_income_ratio.
    Section 16 does not recalculate approved_expected_loss_amount.
    Section 16 does not reassign simulated reason codes.
    Section 16 does not modify rule triggers.
    Section 16 does not modify strategy thresholds.
    Section 16 does not modify product-policy thresholds.

MODULE 3 READINESS BOUNDARY:
    Section 16 captures growth-readiness fields such as:
        approved exposure,
        requested exposure,
        APR proxy,
        term,
        product mix,
        score mix,
        approved Expected Loss,
        and simulated offer-exposure rate.

    These fields will be useful for a future Module 3 economics layer.

    Section 16 does not calculate:
        revenue,
        yield,
        funding cost,
        risk-adjusted margin,
        profitability,
        capital,
        pricing optimization,
        net income,
        lifetime value,
        or risk-adjusted return.

INTERPRETATION BOUNDARIES:
    estimated_pd remains the SQL field name from Module 1 and is interpreted as the synthetic
    estimated-PD proxy.

    requested_expected_loss_amount means requested-exposure Expected Loss from Module 1.

    approved_expected_loss_amount means simulated approved-exposure Expected Loss after Module 2
    exposure treatment.

    approved_expected_loss_rate is a synthetic strategy evidence metric.
    It is not a production loss-rate forecast.

    simulated_offer_exposure_flag means the simulated strategy produced positive offer exposure
    through APPROVE or COUNTEROFFER.

    simulated_offer_exposure_flag does not imply:
        operational account creation,
        booked exposure,
        funded exposure,
        production approval,
        customer treatment,
        or real applicant treatment.

    Reason codes remain simulated strategy explanation codes.

    They are not:
        customer communications,
        regulatory notices,
        adverse-action reasons,
        fair-lending conclusions,
        or production compliance determinations.

VALIDATION STATUS INTERPRETATION:
    PASS:
        The validation check satisfied the expected behavior.

    REVIEW:
        The pattern should be interpreted before documentation.

        REVIEW does not automatically mean the run is invalid.

    NOT_APPLICABLE:
        The test could not be evaluated in the current run context, usually because a comparison,
        archive slice, or relevant subpopulation is unavailable.

OPERATING GUIDANCE — SINGLE RUN:
    For a single completed strategy run:
        1. Run Section 14.
        2. Run Section 15.
        3. Run Section 16.0–16.8.
        4. Review Section 16.8 outputs.
        5. Stop, or proceed to the next selected run.

OPERATING GUIDANCE — FULL CAMPAIGN:
    For a full campaign:
        1. Run each planned strategy slice through Section 16.8.
        2. After all planned archive slices are complete, rerun Section 15 from the completed archive.
        3. Run Section 16.9 to synchronize Section 15-derived frontier fields into persistent
           executive evidence.
        4. Run Section 17 for final campaign acceptance.

COMMON MISUSE TO AVOID:
    Do not treat Section 16 evidence rows as applicant-level outputs.
    Do not treat campaign IDs as replacements for technical run keys.
    Do not treat Section 16.8 diagnostics as final campaign acceptance.
    Do not run Section 16.9 before rerunning Section 15 from the completed campaign archive.
    Do not interpret Section 16 evidence as production decision evidence.
    Do not interpret Module 3 readiness fields as revenue or profitability results.

***************************************************************************************************/


/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 16.0 — CURRENT RUN KEY, REGISTRY STORY METADATA, AND EVIDENCE CAPTURE GATEKEEPER
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Identify the current latest-run Module 2 strategy slice and confirm that evidence capture can be
    performed safely.

    Section 16.0 answers:

        "Which completed strategy run is being captured, what campaign registry IDs identify it,
         and are the upstream validation, frontier, strategy-parameter, product-policy, and story
         helper objects available?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Create tmp_m2_current_run_key from credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions.
    2. Confirm or create a safe temporary campaign-registry match helper.
    3. Create tmp_m2_current_run_story_params using campaign registry values first.
    4. Use fallback story metadata only where registry fallback is allowed.
    5. Validate that credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions exists and is populated.
    6. Validate that the latest-run table resolves to exactly one run key.
    7. Validate that tmp_m2_validation_status_summary exists.
    8. Validate that tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary exists.
    9. Validate that tmp_module2_strategy_params exists.
    10. Validate that tmp_module2_product_policy_params exists.
    11. Validate that exactly one active story-parameter row exists.
    12. Validate registry match status when campaign_registry_required_flag = TRUE.

WHEN TO RUN THIS SECTION:
    Run Section 16.0 after:
        Section 14 has produced tmp_m2_validation_status_summary.
        Section 15 has produced tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary.
        Section 11 has produced credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions.
        Section 3 has preferably produced tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match.

    Section 16.0 can create a temporary fallback registry helper for ad hoc evidence refreshes, but
    formal campaign evidence should be registry matched.

WHAT TO LOOK FOR AFTER THIS SECTION:
    tmp_m2_current_run_key:
        should contain exactly one row.

    tmp_m2_current_run_story_params:
        should contain exactly one active row.

    run_registry_match_status:
        should be RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED for formal campaign runs where
        campaign_registry_required_flag = TRUE.

    registry_match_count:
        should equal 1 for formal campaign runs.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    tmp_m2_current_run_key has zero rows:
        latest-run output is missing or empty.

    tmp_m2_current_run_key has more than one row:
        latest-run output is mixing strategies, scenarios, or populations.

    run_registry_match_status is not RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED when registry is required:
        the code output cannot yet be tied cleanly to the formal campaign roadmap.

    tmp_m2_validation_status_summary is missing:
        Section 14 has not been run in the current session.

    tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary is missing:
        Section 15 has not been run in the current session.

TEMPORARY OUTPUTS:
    tmp_m2_current_run_key
    tmp_m2_current_run_story_params

CURRENT RUN KEY GRAIN:
    One row representing the current completed latest-run strategy slice:

        strategy_set_name
        strategy_name
        strategy_version
        module1_scenario_set_name
        module1_scenario_name
        population_id

WHY CURRENT RUN KEY MUST HAVE EXACTLY ONE ROW:
    Section 16 evidence tables are run-level evidence outputs.

    A mixed latest-run table containing multiple strategies, multiple scenarios, or multiple
    populations would make evidence capture ambiguous.

    Section 16 therefore fails fast if tmp_m2_current_run_key does not contain exactly one row.

WHY CAMPAIGN REGISTRY METADATA IS PRIMARY:
    Formal campaign evidence should tie SQL output to stable run-roadmap IDs.

    Registry IDs make the later Validation Summary easier to trace because they connect:
        technical run key,
        module1_run_id,
        module2_run_id,
        showcase_id,
        comparison_group_id,
        validation_evidence_group_id,
        README storyline label,
        LinkedIn post sequence,
        and validation-summary section hint.

WHY FALLBACK STORY LABELS STILL EXIST:
    Formal campaign registry metadata may not be available during ad hoc development.

    Fallback labels allow evidence capture to proceed only when the strategy parameter allows registry
    fallback.

    For formal campaign documentation, registry-matched evidence is preferred.

DEPENDENCY BOUNDARY:
    Section 16 depends on Section 14 and Section 15 outputs.

    Section 16 does not rebuild those outputs.

    If tmp_m2_validation_status_summary or tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary is missing,
    Section 16 fails clearly rather than capturing incomplete evidence.

SQL CONCEPTS TAUGHT IN THIS SECTION:
    DROP TABLE IF EXISTS:
        Removes stale temporary helpers without failing if they are absent.

    CREATE TEMP TABLE ... AS:
        Materializes a current-session helper table from a SELECT statement.

    SELECT DISTINCT:
        Collapses applicant-grain latest-run rows into a distinct run-key surface.

    TO_REGCLASS:
        Checks whether an object exists before dependent evidence logic uses it.

    ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS:
        Hardens temporary helper schemas across iterative script versions.

    COALESCE:
        Applies registry-first / fallback-second metadata logic.

    CROSS JOIN:
        Safely attaches one-row current-run and story metadata to many validation rows later.

    RAISE EXCEPTION:
        Stops persistent evidence capture when required dependencies or registry conditions are not
        satisfied.

READ-ONLY / PERSISTENCE BOUNDARY:
    Section 16.0 creates temporary helper objects only.

    It does not insert persistent evidence rows.
    It does not delete persistent evidence rows.
    It does not update persistent evidence rows.
    It does not modify credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions.
    It does not modify credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions_archive.
    It does not recalculate decisions.
    It does not recalculate approved exposure.
    It does not recalculate approved Expected Loss.
    It does not reassign simulated reason codes.

***************************************************************************************************/



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.0A Reset current-run evidence helper objects.

Business purpose:
    Remove prior current-run helper objects so Section 16 captures the current latest-run output
    rather than a stale helper table from an earlier session.

SQL purpose:
    Drop only temporary Section 16 helper objects.

SQL mechanics:
    DROP TABLE IF EXISTS is safe for reruns because it does not raise an error when the temporary
    helper object is absent.

Important:
    This section does not drop tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match.

    That helper is created in Section 3 and is the preferred registry source for evidence IDs.

    If the helper is missing, Section 16.0C creates a safe fallback helper so ad hoc development can
    still proceed when registry fallback is allowed.

Boundary:
    This does not delete persistent evidence rows.

    Evidence replacement is handled later by Section 16.2 using the current run key.

What to look for:
    No result grid is expected.

    Successful execution simply clears the two current-run helper objects so they can be rebuilt from
    the current latest-run output.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tmp_m2_current_run_key;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS tmp_m2_current_run_story_params;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.0B Materialize the current latest-run key.

Business purpose:
    Convert the current latest-run output into a one-row run key that all Section 16 evidence inserts
    can join against.

SQL purpose:
    Pull distinct strategy, Module 1 lineage, archive-control, source, and timestamp fields from the
    latest-run output.

Credit risk / governance interpretation:
    This run key is the evidence grain.

    It says:
        "This evidence belongs to this Module 2 strategy applied to this Module 1 scenario and
         population."

    It does not identify individual applicants.

Inline SQL logic:
    SELECT DISTINCT is used because credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions is applicant-grain.

    Every applicant row should carry the same strategy identity and Module 1 scenario lineage.

    If more than one distinct run key exists, the gatekeeper below fails because evidence capture
    would be ambiguous.

What to look for:
    This table should contain exactly one row after creation.

Review trigger:
    More than one row means the latest-run table is mixing technical run keys.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_current_run_key
ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS
AS
SELECT DISTINCT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 2 strategy identity.

    These fields identify the downstream strategy being captured.

    strategy_run_id and strategy_run_ts support audit traceability but do not replace the technical
    evidence grain.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    module2_model_version,
    strategy_set_name,
    strategy_name,
    strategy_version,
    strategy_run_id,
    strategy_run_ts,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Archive and final-resolution controls.

    These fields explain how the run was persisted and how failed preliminary counteroffers were
    resolved.

    They are captured because they affect interpretation of the completed strategy run.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    archive_run_flag,
    strategy_archive_reset_mode,
    failed_counteroffer_resolution,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 1 source mode.

    These fields document whether Module 2 consumed latest-run Module 1 output or an archived Module
    1 scenario slice.

    ARCHIVE source is preferred for formal campaign comparison because it preserves the upstream
    scenario slice.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    module1_input_source,
    module1_source_table_name,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 1 lineage.

    These fields identify the upstream synthetic population and scenario environment.

    They must remain separate from Module 2 strategy identity.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    module1_model_version,
    module1_scenario_set_name,
    module1_scenario_name,
    population_id,
    anchor_date,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Latest-run materialization timestamp.

    Audit metadata only.

    This is not a strategy-comparison key.
    This is not an archive replacement key.
    This is not a business outcome.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    latest_output_created_ts

FROM credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.0C Ensure current-run campaign registry helper is available.

Business purpose:
    Make Section 16 robust whether it is run immediately after Section 3 or during ad hoc evidence
    refresh work.

SQL purpose:
    If tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match does not exist, create a temporary fallback helper
    with the registry fields Section 16 expects.

    If the helper exists but lacks newer registry fields, add those fields.

    If the helper has no rows, insert one fallback status row scoped to the current run key.

Formal campaign behavior:
    If campaign_registry_required_flag = TRUE, the gatekeeper below still requires:

        run_registry_match_status = 'RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED'

    Therefore this fallback helper does not hide missing formal registry mapping.

Ad hoc behavior:
    If campaign_registry_required_flag = FALSE, fallback metadata is allowed and evidence capture can
    proceed with explicit fallback status.

SQL mechanics:
    TO_REGCLASS checks whether the temporary helper exists.

    CREATE TEMP TABLE creates the helper only when absent.

    ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS makes the helper compatible with the current Section 16
    evidence insert logic even if an older Section 3 created a narrower helper.

    The fallback INSERT is guarded by IF NOT EXISTS so a real Section 3 registry match is preserved.

What to look for:
    In formal campaign execution, this helper should already have a registry-matched row from
    Section 3.

    In ad hoc execution, this block may create a fallback row with
    RUN_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND_FALLBACK_ALLOWED.

Review trigger:
    RUN_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND_REQUIRED_REVIEW means campaign_registry_required_flag is TRUE but the run
    did not match the registry. Section 16.0E will stop evidence capture.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

DO $$
BEGIN
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Create a safe fallback registry helper only when Section 3 did not create one in the current
    session.

    The table shape mirrors the fields Section 16 consumes.

    Important:
        This is not the persistent registry table.
        This is a temporary helper used only for current-run evidence capture.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    IF COALESCE(
        TO_REGCLASS('pg_temp.tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match'),
        TO_REGCLASS('tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match')
    ) IS NULL THEN

        CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match (
            campaign_registry_required_flag         BOOLEAN,

            module1_scenario_set_name              TEXT,
            module1_scenario_name                  TEXT,
            population_id                          TEXT,

            strategy_set_name                      TEXT,
            strategy_name                          TEXT,
            strategy_version                       TEXT,

            registry_match_count                   BIGINT,
            run_registry_match_status              TEXT,

            module1_run_id                         TEXT,
            module2_run_id                         TEXT,
            run_sequence_number                    INTEGER,

            showcase_id                            TEXT,
            showcase_name                          TEXT,
            comparison_group_id                    TEXT,
            validation_evidence_group_id           TEXT,

            module1_parameter_family_changed        TEXT,
            module1_parameter_change_summary        TEXT,

            module2_strategy_family_changed         TEXT,
            module2_parameter_change_summary        TEXT,

            expected_storyline                     TEXT,
            validation_summary_section_hint         TEXT,
            readme_storyline_label                 TEXT,
            linkedin_post_sequence                 INTEGER
        ) ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS;
    END IF;


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Schema hardening for the temporary registry helper.

    SQL mechanics:
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS makes this block safe to run even when the helper already has the
        column.

    Why this matters:
        CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS and temp-helper reuse can leave older helper shapes in place.
        These ALTER statements prevent later INSERT / SELECT references from failing because a
        registry-story field is absent.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS campaign_registry_required_flag BOOLEAN;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module1_scenario_set_name TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module1_scenario_name TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS population_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS strategy_set_name TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS strategy_name TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS strategy_version TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS registry_match_count BIGINT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS run_registry_match_status TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module1_run_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module2_run_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS run_sequence_number INTEGER;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS showcase_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS showcase_name TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS comparison_group_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS validation_evidence_group_id TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module1_parameter_family_changed TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module1_parameter_change_summary TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module2_strategy_family_changed TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS module2_parameter_change_summary TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS expected_storyline TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS validation_summary_section_hint TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS readme_storyline_label TEXT;

    ALTER TABLE tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
        ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS linkedin_post_sequence INTEGER;


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Insert a fallback row only if the helper is empty.

    This preserves Section 3 output when it exists.

    SQL mechanics:
        IF NOT EXISTS prevents the fallback row from duplicating or overwriting a real registry match.

        CROSS JOIN attaches the one active strategy parameter row to the one current run key.

    Interpretation:
        registry_match_count = 0 is intentionally explicit.

        The fallback status remains visible in evidence rows so reviewers know whether the run was
        registry-matched or fallback-labeled.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    IF NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1
        FROM tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
    ) THEN
        INSERT INTO tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match (
            campaign_registry_required_flag,

            module1_scenario_set_name,
            module1_scenario_name,
            population_id,

            strategy_set_name,
            strategy_name,
            strategy_version,

            registry_match_count,
            run_registry_match_status
        )
        SELECT
            COALESCE(p.campaign_registry_required_flag, FALSE),

            k.module1_scenario_set_name,
            k.module1_scenario_name,
            k.population_id,

            k.strategy_set_name,
            k.strategy_name,
            k.strategy_version,

            0::BIGINT AS registry_match_count,

            CASE
                WHEN COALESCE(p.campaign_registry_required_flag, FALSE) IS TRUE
                    THEN 'RUN_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND_REQUIRED_REVIEW'
                ELSE 'RUN_REGISTRY_NOT_FOUND_FALLBACK_ALLOWED'
            END AS run_registry_match_status

        FROM tmp_m2_current_run_key k

        CROSS JOIN (
            SELECT
                campaign_registry_required_flag
            FROM tmp_module2_strategy_params
            WHERE active_strategy_flag IS TRUE
            LIMIT 1
        ) p

        LIMIT 1;
    END IF;
END $$;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.0D Build temporary current-run story metadata.

Business purpose:
    Attach human-readable campaign / story labels to the technical run key.

Patch behavior:
    Registry fields from tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match are now the primary source.

    Fallback labels are used only where registry fields are NULL.

SQL purpose:
    Derive run IDs, showcase labels, parameter-family labels, expected storylines, documentation
    labels, validation evidence IDs, and registry match status.

Why this is still temporary:
    The purpose is evidence capture support.

    It does not modify the archive.
    It does not modify strategy decisions.
    It does not change strategy thresholds.

Design note:
    Formal campaign runs should show:

        run_registry_match_status = 'RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED'

    Ad hoc runs may show fallback status only when the active strategy allows registry fallback.

SQL mechanics:
    k CTE:
        loads the one current run key.

    reg CTE:
        loads the current registry-match helper row.

    fallback_labels CTE:
        derives readable fallback labels using CASE expressions.

    Final SELECT:
        uses COALESCE to prefer registry metadata first and fallback labels second.

What to look for:
    active_story_flag should be TRUE.

    campaign_registry_required_flag should match the active strategy parameter.

    run_registry_match_status should be RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED for formal runs.

Review trigger:
    Missing module2_run_id or validation_evidence_group_id on a formal run indicates the campaign
    registry linkage should be reviewed before documentation.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

CREATE TEMP TABLE tmp_m2_current_run_story_params
ON COMMIT PRESERVE ROWS
AS
WITH k AS (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    k:
        Current run key.

    SQL mechanics:
        LIMIT 1 is safe because Section 16.0E validates that tmp_m2_current_run_key has exactly one
        row before persistent evidence rows are inserted.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT *
    FROM tmp_m2_current_run_key
    LIMIT 1
),

reg AS (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    reg:
        Registry-match helper.

    Interpretation:
        Prefer real Section 3 registry values when they exist.

        If Section 3 did not provide the helper, Section 16.0C creates a fallback row.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    SELECT *
    FROM tmp_m2_campaign_registry_current_run_match
    LIMIT 1
),

fallback_labels AS (
    SELECT
        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback showcase label.

        Used only if registry showcase_name is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name IN (
                    'BASELINE_V2',
                    'CREDIT_BOX_BASELINE_REFERENCE',
                    'PORTFOLIO_MIX_BASELINE_REFERENCE',
                    'EXPOSURE_BASELINE_REFERENCE',
                    'LGD_BASELINE_REFERENCE'
                 )
                THEN 'BASELINE_REFERENCE'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%RATE%'
                THEN 'RATE_SENSITIVITY_SHOWCASE'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SCORE%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%PRIME%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%CREDIT_STRESS%'
                THEN 'CREDIT_BOX_SHOWCASE'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%MIX%'
                THEN 'PORTFOLIO_MIX_SHOWCASE'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%BALANCE%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%EXPOSURE%'
                THEN 'EXPOSURE_INTENSITY_SHOWCASE'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%LGD%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SEVERITY%'
                THEN 'SEVERITY_SENSITIVITY_SHOWCASE'

            ELSE 'UNCLASSIFIED_SHOWCASE'
        END AS fallback_showcase_name,


        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback Module 1 parameter family.

        Used only if registry module1_parameter_family_changed is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%RATE_UP%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%RATE_DOWN%'
                THEN 'RATE_ENVIRONMENT'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SCORE%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%PRIME%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%CREDIT_STRESS%'
                THEN 'SCORE_MIX'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%PRODUCT%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%UNSECURED%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%REVOLVING%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SECURED%'
                THEN 'PRODUCT_MIX'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%BALANCE%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%EXPOSURE%'
                THEN 'EXPOSURE_INTENSITY'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%LGD%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SEVERITY%'
                THEN 'LGD_SEVERITY'

            ELSE 'BASELINE_OR_UNCLASSIFIED'
        END AS fallback_module1_parameter_family_changed,


        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback Module 1 parameter summary.

        Used only if registry module1_parameter_change_summary is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name = 'BASELINE_V2'
                THEN 'Module 1 baseline scenario using accepted V2.0 defaults.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%RATE_UP%'
                THEN 'Module 1 rate-up sensitivity scenario.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%RATE_DOWN%'
                THEN 'Module 1 rate-down sensitivity scenario.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%PRIME%'
                THEN 'Module 1 score-mix scenario emphasizing prime or near-prime composition.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%UNSECURED%'
                THEN 'Module 1 product-mix or severity scenario emphasizing unsecured exposure.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%REVOLVING%'
                THEN 'Module 1 product-mix scenario emphasizing revolving exposure.'

            WHEN k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%SEVERITY%'
              OR k.module1_scenario_name ILIKE '%LGD%'
                THEN 'Module 1 LGD / severity sensitivity scenario.'

            ELSE 'Module 1 scenario parameter change summary not manually specified.'
        END AS fallback_module1_parameter_change_summary,


        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback Module 2 strategy family.

        Used only if registry module2_strategy_family_changed is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'BASELINE'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'TIGHTER_SYNTHETIC_RISK_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'RISK_TIGHTENING'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'GROWTH_ORIENTED_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'GROWTH'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'TIGHTER_AFFORDABILITY_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'AFFORDABILITY_TIGHTENING'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'COUNTEROFFER_FIRST_EXPOSURE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'COUNTEROFFER_MANAGEMENT'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'PRODUCT_SPECIFIC_GUARDRAIL_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'PRODUCT_GUARDRAILS'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'MANUAL_REVIEW_REDUCTION_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'OPERATIONS_REVIEW_REDUCTION'

            ELSE 'UNCLASSIFIED_STRATEGY'
        END AS fallback_module2_strategy_family_changed,


        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback Module 2 parameter summary.

        Used only if registry module2_parameter_change_summary is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 baseline strategy using accepted V1.0 defaults.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'TIGHTER_SYNTHETIC_RISK_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy tightens synthetic estimated-PD proxy tolerance thresholds.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'GROWTH_ORIENTED_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy loosens selected synthetic-risk, PTI, and DTI tolerances for growth-oriented comparison.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'TIGHTER_AFFORDABILITY_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy tightens PTI thresholds and counteroffer target PTI.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'COUNTEROFFER_FIRST_EXPOSURE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy emphasizes reduced-exposure treatment through tighter approved amount, approved EL, and PTI constraints.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'PRODUCT_SPECIFIC_GUARDRAIL_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy applies tighter guardrails to selected unsecured / revolving products.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'MANUAL_REVIEW_REDUCTION_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Module 2 strategy resolves failed counteroffers to final no-offer outcomes to reduce unresolved manual review.'

            ELSE 'Module 2 strategy parameter change summary not manually specified.'
        END AS fallback_module2_parameter_change_summary,


        /*******************************************************************************************
        Fallback expected storyline.

        Used only if registry expected_storyline is unavailable.
        *******************************************************************************************/
        CASE
            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'BASELINE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Establish reference strategy behavior for this Module 1 scenario.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'TIGHTER_SYNTHETIC_RISK_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Demonstrate risk-discipline tradeoff: lower simulated offer exposure and approved Expected Loss, with higher decline rate.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'GROWTH_ORIENTED_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Demonstrate growth tradeoff: higher simulated offer exposure and approved amount, with measurable risk impact.'

            WHEN k.strategy_name = 'COUNTEROFFER_FIRST_EXPOSURE_STRATEGY'
                THEN 'Demonstrate exposure-management tradeoff through reduced approved amount, improved PTI, and lower approved Expected Loss.'

            ELSE 'Expected storyline not manually specified.'
        END AS fallback_expected_storyline

    FROM k
)

SELECT
    /***********************************************************************************************
    One active story row is expected.

    The Section 16 gatekeeper validates this cardinality.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    TRUE AS active_story_flag,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Registry governance status.

    These fields make evidence rows clear about whether they are registry-matched or fallback-based.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(reg.campaign_registry_required_flag, FALSE) AS campaign_registry_required_flag,
    COALESCE(reg.registry_match_count, 0) AS registry_match_count,
    COALESCE(reg.run_registry_match_status, 'RUN_REGISTRY_NOT_AVAILABLE_SECTION3_NOT_RUN')
        AS run_registry_match_status,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Campaign run IDs.

    Registry values are preferred.

    Fallback values are readable but should not be used as final campaign evidence IDs when formal
    registry matching is required.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(reg.module1_run_id, k.module1_scenario_name) AS module1_run_id,

    COALESCE(
        reg.module2_run_id,
        k.module1_scenario_name || '__' || k.strategy_name
    ) AS module2_run_id,

    reg.run_sequence_number,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Showcase and comparison identifiers.

    These fields support README, LinkedIn, comparison, and Validation Summary linkage.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    reg.showcase_id,
    COALESCE(reg.showcase_name, f.fallback_showcase_name) AS showcase_name,
    reg.comparison_group_id,
    reg.validation_evidence_group_id,

    reg.readme_storyline_label,
    reg.linkedin_post_sequence,
    reg.validation_summary_section_hint,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 1 scenario story fields.

    Registry values are preferred because they align to the formal campaign roadmap.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(
        reg.module1_parameter_family_changed,
        f.fallback_module1_parameter_family_changed
    ) AS module1_parameter_family_changed,

    COALESCE(
        reg.module1_parameter_change_summary,
        f.fallback_module1_parameter_change_summary
    ) AS module1_parameter_change_summary,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 2 strategy story fields.

    Registry values are preferred because they align to the formal campaign roadmap.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(
        reg.module2_strategy_family_changed,
        f.fallback_module2_strategy_family_changed
    ) AS module2_strategy_family_changed,

    COALESCE(
        reg.module2_parameter_change_summary,
        f.fallback_module2_parameter_change_summary
    ) AS module2_parameter_change_summary,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Expected storyline.

    Registry value is preferred.
    Fallback value remains available for development runs.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    COALESCE(reg.expected_storyline, f.fallback_expected_storyline) AS expected_storyline,


    /***********************************************************************************************
    Reviewer-populated narrative fields.

    These intentionally start NULL.

    observed_storyline may be filled from Section 15 frontier interpretation in Section 16.6.
    validation_notes may be manually populated later after QA review.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    NULL::TEXT AS observed_storyline,
    NULL::TEXT AS validation_notes

FROM k
LEFT JOIN reg
    ON TRUE
CROSS JOIN fallback_labels f
LIMIT 1;



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.0E Evidence capture fail-fast gatekeeper.

Business purpose:
    Prevent incomplete or ambiguous evidence capture.

Why fail fast:
    Evidence capture is persistent.

    It is better to stop than to create evidence rows with:
        no latest-run output,
        multiple run keys,
        missing validation summary,
        missing frontier summary,
        missing parameter tables,
        ambiguous story metadata,
        or missing required campaign registry mapping.

SQL logic:
    This block validates object existence, row counts, run-key cardinality, dependency availability,
    story-row cardinality, and registry match requirements before Section 16 creates or replaces
    persistent evidence rows.

What to look for:
    Successful execution produces no result grid.

    It either completes silently or raises a clear exception.

Review triggers:
    Any exception in this block means evidence capture should not proceed until the missing dependency
    or ambiguous scope issue is resolved.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

DO $$
DECLARE
    v_latest_table_exists BOOLEAN;
    v_latest_row_count BIGINT;
    v_current_key_count BIGINT;
    v_validation_summary_exists BOOLEAN;
    v_frontier_summary_exists BOOLEAN;
    v_strategy_params_exists BOOLEAN;
    v_product_policy_params_exists BOOLEAN;
    v_story_param_count BIGINT;

    v_campaign_registry_required_flag BOOLEAN;
    v_run_registry_match_status TEXT;
    v_registry_match_count BIGINT;
BEGIN
    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: latest-run output table must exist.

    Section 16 captures evidence from the completed latest-run output created in Section 11.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT TO_REGCLASS('credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions') IS NOT NULL
    INTO v_latest_table_exists;

    IF v_latest_table_exists IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions does not exist.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: latest-run output must contain rows.

    Evidence capture from an empty latest-run output would create misleading documentation artifacts.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COUNT(*)
    INTO v_latest_row_count
    FROM credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_decisions;

    IF v_latest_row_count <= 0 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: latest-run strategy_decisions table contains zero rows.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: current run key must resolve to exactly one strategy / scenario / population slice.

    If this count is greater than one, the latest-run table is mixed and Section 16 cannot safely
    create one evidence record.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COUNT(*)
    INTO v_current_key_count
    FROM tmp_m2_current_run_key;

    IF v_current_key_count <> 1 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: expected exactly one current latest-run strategy / Module 1 scenario / population key, found %.',
            v_current_key_count;
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: Section 14 validation summary must exist.

    Section 16 does not rebuild validation evidence. It persists Section 14 output.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COALESCE(
        TO_REGCLASS('pg_temp.tmp_m2_validation_status_summary'),
        TO_REGCLASS('tmp_m2_validation_status_summary')
    ) IS NOT NULL
    INTO v_validation_summary_exists;

    IF v_validation_summary_exists IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: tmp_m2_validation_status_summary is required before evidence capture.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: Section 15 frontier executive summary must exist.

    Section 16 later uses frontier fields for executive story capture. Failing here prevents partial
    evidence capture without frontier context.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COALESCE(
        TO_REGCLASS('pg_temp.tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary'),
        TO_REGCLASS('tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary')
    ) IS NOT NULL
    INTO v_frontier_summary_exists;

    IF v_frontier_summary_exists IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: tmp_m2_strategy_frontier_executive_summary is required before evidence capture.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: active strategy parameter card must exist.

    Section 16 snapshots the parameter card so future documentation can trace evidence back to the
    exact strategy configuration.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COALESCE(
        TO_REGCLASS('pg_temp.tmp_module2_strategy_params'),
        TO_REGCLASS('tmp_module2_strategy_params')
    ) IS NOT NULL
    INTO v_strategy_params_exists;

    IF v_strategy_params_exists IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: tmp_module2_strategy_params is required for parameter snapshot capture.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: product-policy parameter card must exist.

    Product-policy parameters are critical for explaining approved exposure, counteroffer feasibility,
    approved Expected Loss, and product × score segment evidence.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COALESCE(
        TO_REGCLASS('pg_temp.tmp_module2_product_policy_params'),
        TO_REGCLASS('tmp_module2_product_policy_params')
    ) IS NOT NULL
    INTO v_product_policy_params_exists;

    IF v_product_policy_params_exists IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: tmp_module2_product_policy_params is required for product-policy parameter snapshot capture.';
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: exactly one active story-parameter row.

    The story helper should produce one and only one evidence-story row for the current run key.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT COUNT(*)
    INTO v_story_param_count
    FROM tmp_m2_current_run_story_params
    WHERE active_story_flag IS TRUE;

    IF v_story_param_count <> 1 THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: expected exactly one active story-parameter row, found %.',
            v_story_param_count;
    END IF;


    /*-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Gatekeeper: registry match status when formal campaign registry is required.

    Formal campaign evidence should not silently fall back to generated labels.

    If campaign_registry_required_flag = TRUE, Section 16 requires:
        run_registry_match_status = RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED
        registry_match_count = 1

    This protects the connection from code output to validation-summary IDs.
    -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
    SELECT
        COALESCE(campaign_registry_required_flag, FALSE),
        COALESCE(registry_match_count, 0),
        COALESCE(run_registry_match_status, '<NULL>')
    INTO
        v_campaign_registry_required_flag,
        v_registry_match_count,
        v_run_registry_match_status
    FROM tmp_m2_current_run_story_params
    WHERE active_story_flag IS TRUE
    LIMIT 1;

    IF v_campaign_registry_required_flag IS TRUE
       AND (
            v_run_registry_match_status <> 'RUN_REGISTRY_MATCHED'
            OR v_registry_match_count <> 1
       ) THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION
            'Module 2 Section 16 failure: formal campaign registry is required, but current run is not registry-matched. Status: %, match count: %.',
            v_run_registry_match_status,
            v_registry_match_count;
    END IF;
END $$;



/***************************************************************************************************
SECTION 16.1 — CREATE PERSISTENT EVIDENCE TABLES
****************************************************************************************************

BUSINESS PURPOSE:
    Create persistent tables that preserve run-level executive evidence, validation evidence,
    parameter snapshots, and product × score segment evidence.

    Section 16.1 answers:

        "Where will the project store durable evidence for this completed strategy run?"

SQL PURPOSE:
    1. Create credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_evidence_summary if it does not exist.
    2. Create credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_validation_evidence if it does not exist.
    3. Create credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_parameter_snapshot if it does not exist.
    4. Create credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_segment_evidence_summary if it does not exist.
    5. Add campaign registry ID columns to existing evidence tables when needed.
    6. Create practical evidence-table indexes.

PERSISTENT TABLE PURPOSES:
    strategy_run_evidence_summary:
        One row per completed strategy / Module 1 scenario / population run.
        Stores executive story, outcome metrics, exposure metrics, risk metrics, validation counts,
        frontier classification, reason-code story fields, run registry fields, comparison IDs, and
        Module 3 readiness flags.

    strategy_run_validation_evidence:
        One row per completed run × validation test.
        Stores Section 14 validation status details plus run registry identifiers.

    strategy_run_parameter_snapshot:
        One row per completed run.
        Stores JSONB snapshots of active strategy parameters, product policy parameters, and run-story
        metadata plus run registry identifiers.

    strategy_run_segment_evidence_summary:
        One row per completed run × product_type_policy_key × score_band_policy_key.
        Stores segment evidence for product × score storytelling and future strategy analysis plus
        run registry identifiers.

GRAIN:
    Main executive evidence:
        one row per Module 1 scenario × Module 2 strategy version

    Validation evidence:
        one row per run × validation test

    Parameter snapshot:
        one row per run

    Segment evidence:
        one row per run × product_type_policy_key × score_band_policy_key

WHAT TO LOOK FOR AFTER THIS SECTION:
    The four persistent evidence tables should exist.

    Existing evidence tables from prior development versions should receive the registry-linkage
    columns through ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS.

    Indexes should be created or reused.

REVIEW TRIGGERS:
    If later INSERT statements fail because a column is missing, the schema-hardening block should be
    checked first.

    If unique index creation fails, existing duplicate evidence rows may exist and should be reviewed
    before evidence capture proceeds.

WHY CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS IS USED:
    Evidence tables should persist across the campaign.

    They should not be dropped during ordinary reruns.

SCHEMA EVOLUTION NOTE:
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS does not alter existing table schemas.

    This patched section therefore also includes ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS statements to
    add registry-linkage fields to evidence tables that may already exist from a prior Section 16
    version.

BOUNDARY:
    This section creates and hardens evidence tables only.

    It does not insert evidence rows yet.
    It does not modify latest-run output.
    It does not modify archive output.
    It does not calculate strategy metrics.

SQL CONCEPTS TAUGHT IN THIS SECTION:
    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS:
        Creates persistent evidence tables on first run and reuses them on later runs.

    ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS:
        Adds new registry fields to older evidence tables without destroying existing rows.

    CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS:
        Adds lookup and uniqueness support for evidence retrieval and replacement.

    UNIQUE INDEX:
        Enforces one evidence row per run key where the evidence table is intended to be one-row-per-run.

***************************************************************************************************/



/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
16.1A Create one-row executive evidence summary table.

Business purpose:
    Store the executive story and primary run-level metrics for each completed run.

Column grouping:
    Columns are intentionally grouped by evidence identity, registry metadata, story metadata,
    strategy identity, Module 1 lineage, outcome metrics, exposure metrics, growth-readiness fields,
    risk / Expected Loss metrics, reason-code fields, frontier fields, validation summary fields,
    segment driver fields, and Module 3 readiness fields.

What to look for:
    This table should have one row per completed strategy / Module 1 scenario / population key after
    Section 16.6.

Interpretation:
    This is the main executive evidence register.

    It is not the row-level applicant output.
    It is not the archive.
    It is not a production reporting mart.
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS credit_decisioning_sim.strategy_run_evidence_summary (
    /***********************************************************************************************
    Evidence snapshot identity.

    evidence_snapshot_id is generated at capture time.
    It supports traceability but is not the run-key grain.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    evidence_snapshot_ts TIMESTAMP,
    evidence_snapshot_id TEXT,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Campaign registry and story metadata.

    These fields link SQL evidence to the run roadmap, README / LinkedIn storyline, and Validation
    Summary structure.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    campaign_registry_required_flag BOOLEAN,
    registry_match_count BIGINT,
    run_registry_match_status TEXT,

    module1_run_id TEXT,
    module2_run_id TEXT,
    run_sequence_number INTEGER,

    showcase_id TEXT,
    showcase_name TEXT,
    comparison_group_id TEXT,
    validation_evidence_group_id TEXT,

    readme_storyline_label TEXT,
    linkedin_post_sequence INTEGER,
    validation_summary_section_hint TEXT,

    module1_parameter_family_changed TEXT,
    module1_parameter_change_summary TEXT,
    module2_strategy_family_changed TEXT,
    module2_parameter_change_summary TEXT,
    expected_storyline TEXT,
    observed_storyline TEXT,
    validation_notes TEXT,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 2 strategy identity.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    module2_model_version TEXT,
    strategy_set_name TEXT,
    strategy_name TEXT,
    strategy_version TEXT,
    strategy_run_id TEXT,
    strategy_run_ts TIMESTAMP,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Output / archive timestamp metadata.

    These fields support traceability only.
    They are not comparison keys and not business outcomes.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    latest_output_created_ts TIMESTAMP,
    archive_inserted_ts TIMESTAMP,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Archive controls and final-resolution setting.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    archive_run_flag BOOLEAN,
    strategy_archive_reset_mode TEXT,
    failed_counteroffer_resolution TEXT,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 1 source mode and lineage.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    module1_input_source TEXT,
    module1_source_table_name TEXT,

    module1_model_version TEXT,
    module1_scenario_set_name TEXT,
    module1_scenario_name TEXT,
    population_id TEXT,
    anchor_date DATE,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Structural run counts.

    duplicate_application_count should be zero for accepted evidence.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    applications BIGINT,
    distinct_application_id_count BIGINT,
    duplicate_application_count BIGINT,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Final decision outcome counts.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    approval_count BIGINT,
    counteroffer_count BIGINT,
    manual_review_count BIGINT,
    decline_count BIGINT,
    offer_count BIGINT,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Final decision outcome rates.

    simulated_offer_exposure_rate = APPROVE + COUNTEROFFER share.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    approval_rate NUMERIC,
    counteroffer_rate NUMERIC,
    manual_review_rate NUMERIC,
    decline_rate NUMERIC,
    simulated_offer_exposure_rate NUMERIC,
    approval_share_of_offers NUMERIC,
    counteroffer_share_of_offers NUMERIC,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Requested and approved exposure metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    total_requested_amount NUMERIC,
    total_approved_amount NUMERIC,
    approved_amount_capture_rate NUMERIC,

    avg_requested_amount NUMERIC,
    avg_approved_amount_all_apps NUMERIC,
    avg_approved_amount_offer_rows NUMERIC,

    total_exposure_reduction_amount NUMERIC,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Counteroffer, manual-review, and decline exposure context.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    counteroffer_requested_amount NUMERIC,
    counteroffer_approved_amount NUMERIC,
    counteroffer_exposure_reduction_amount NUMERIC,
    counteroffer_exposure_reduction_pct_avg NUMERIC,

    manual_review_requested_amount NUMERIC,
    manual_review_requested_expected_loss_amount NUMERIC,
    decline_requested_amount NUMERIC,
    decline_requested_expected_loss_amount NUMERIC,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Requested and approved affordability metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    avg_requested_payment_to_income_ratio NUMERIC,
    avg_approved_payment_to_income_ratio_offer_rows NUMERIC,

    /***********************************************************************************************
    Module 3 growth-readiness APR proxy metrics.

    These fields preserve inputs useful for future economics design.

    They are not revenue or profitability metrics.
    ***********************************************************************************************/
    avg_apr_proxy_all_apps NUMERIC,
    avg_apr_proxy_offer_rows NUMERIC,
    requested_amount_weighted_apr_proxy NUMERIC,
    approved_amount_weighted_apr_proxy NUMERIC,
